    <title>Announcing Peaseblossom: an Oberon-2 compiler, and libraries and programs for it and for voc</title>
    <id>https://tkurtbond.github.io/posts/2026/11/17/announcing-peaseblossom-an-oberon-2-compiler-and-libraries-and-programs-for-it-and-for-voc/</id>
    <updated>2026-11-17T16:48:47-05:00</updated>
    <published>2026-11-17T16:48:47-05:00</published>
    <author>
      <name>T. Kurt Bond</name>
    </author>
    <link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://tkurtbond.github.io/posts/2026/11/17/announcing-peaseblossom-an-oberon-2-compiler-and-libraries-and-programs-for-it-and-for-voc/"/>
    <summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;This software was developed with the aid of AI.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I'm announcing a set of Oberon-2 projects: a compiler, libraries, and
programs built with them. All are on GitHub, and the software is under
the BSD 3-Clause License.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;section id="for-voc-vishap-oberon"&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;For voc (Vishap Oberon)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul class="simple"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/olibfyaml"&gt;olibfyaml&lt;/a&gt;: a binding to &lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/pantoniou/libfyaml"&gt;libfyaml&lt;/a&gt;, the YAML 1.2 parser and
emitter. It covers parsing, navigation, typed values, building and
emitting YAML or JSON, anchors, tags and source locations. Errors in
the input come back as values with &lt;code class="docutils literal"&gt;file:line:column&lt;/code&gt; messages.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
&lt;section id="for-both-voc-and-poc"&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;For both voc and poc&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul class="simple"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/opp"&gt;opp&lt;/a&gt;: the Oberon pretty printer. It reformats modules within a
maximum line length and keeps every comment. It handles both
compilers' language extensions. Before writing, it checks that its
output has exactly the tokens and comments of its input.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/oberon-ropes"&gt;Ropes&lt;/a&gt;: ropes, strings represented as trees of pieces, with a
self-test and a command-line demo.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/oberon-argparser"&gt;ArgParser&lt;/a&gt;: command-line parsing: long and short options,
clustered short options, option values, commands, and usage messages.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
&lt;section id="peaseblossom"&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Peaseblossom&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul class="simple"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/peaseblossom"&gt;poc&lt;/a&gt;, the Peaseblossom Oberon Compiler: an Oberon-2 compiler with
an LLVM backend for Linux, FreeBSD, OpenBSD and NetBSD, and a VAX
backend for VAX/VMS 5.5-2. It was motivated in large part by my wish
for a more modern language than the limited ones available on the VAX
system I maintain. A release tarball needs only clang and GNU make,
or it can be bootstrapped using Vishap Oberon. Packages are available
for Fedora, Debian, FreeBSD, OpenBSD and NetBSD. It comes with a
User's Guide and a Reference Guide.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/polibfyaml"&gt;polibfyaml&lt;/a&gt;: olibfyaml for poc, with the same API, tests and
examples.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/ova_fmt_po"&gt;ova_fmt_po&lt;/a&gt;, an example program using polibfyaml: it formats
characters for the OVA tabletop RPG from YAML as reStructuredText,
gemtext, Markdown and more.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/pofltk"&gt;pofltk&lt;/a&gt;: a thick binding to &lt;a class="reference external" href="https://www.fltk.org"&gt;FLTK&lt;/a&gt; 1.4. FLTK's classes are record
types a program extends, and &lt;code class="docutils literal"&gt;draw()&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code class="docutils literal"&gt;handle()&lt;/code&gt; are
type-bound procedures it overrides. Coverage includes widgets, layout
(flex and grid), menus, dialogs, text editors, browsers, trees,
tables, images, the clipboard and preferences. &lt;code class="docutils literal"&gt;FlTest&lt;/code&gt; drives a
program's windows in tests on Xvfb or a headless compositor. Many of
FLTK's test programs are ported as examples.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/ova_cg_po"&gt;ova_cg_po&lt;/a&gt;, an example program using pofltk: a character
generator for OVA. It's a complete program with a menu bar, tabs,
grids, file choosers, preferences and window tests. Its &lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/ova_cg_po/blob/main/doc/pofltk-tour.md"&gt;tour for
pofltk readers&lt;/a&gt; says where in its code each technique is.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/poDateTime"&gt;poDateTime&lt;/a&gt;: dates, times, durations and time zones. It has
instants, the proleptic Gregorian calendar, clocks, POSIX &lt;code class="docutils literal"&gt;TZ&lt;/code&gt; rules
and tz database files, ISO 8601, RFC 3339 and &lt;code class="docutils literal"&gt;strftime&lt;/code&gt; formats. It
runs on the four Unixes and on VAX/VMS.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/pounix"&gt;pounix&lt;/a&gt;: Unix services that poc's runtime doesn't provide:
dropping root for good, file descriptors and directories, running
child processes with an argument list (no shell), and &lt;code class="docutils literal"&gt;errno&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/posockets"&gt;posockets&lt;/a&gt;: TCP (IPv4 and IPv6) and Unix-domain sockets. It works
with poc's cooperative &lt;code class="docutils literal"&gt;Tasks&lt;/code&gt;, so one thread can serve many
connections, with deadlines.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/polibtls"&gt;polibtls&lt;/a&gt;: a binding to libtls (LibreTLS on Linux, FreeBSD and
NetBSD; OpenBSD's own). It supports server contexts with several key
pairs, client certificates, and non-blocking I/O with &lt;code class="docutils literal"&gt;Tasks&lt;/code&gt;. It's
tested against openssl, gnutls-cli and Go's TLS.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/pogemini"&gt;pogemini&lt;/a&gt;: a &lt;a class="reference external" href="https://geminiprotocol.net/"&gt;Gemini&lt;/a&gt; server built on the four libraries above. It
serves several capsules by host name, each with its own certificate,
and can require client certificates for parts of a capsule. Every
connection runs in a task in one thread.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="reference external" href="https://github.com/tkurtbond/poDB"&gt;poDB&lt;/a&gt;: a database-independent SQL interface, with drivers for
SQLite and PostgreSQL. A program moves from one to the other by
changing its connection URL and the driver it imports.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Comments, bug reports and patches are welcome. Many of the poc
libraries are early in their lives; polibfyaml and pofltk have had the
most use.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;</summary>
